            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"Exile, separation from God's presence, and judgment.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Genesis 3:24\",\n    \"Genesis 4:16\",\n    \"Genesis 11:2\",\n    \"Genesis 13:11\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The arrival of God's glory, divine light, and the coming or return of Christ.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Ezekiel 43:2\",\n        \"Matthew 2:1-2\",\n        \"Matthew 24:27\",\n        \"Zechariah 14:4\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Paganism, idolatry, and foreign worldly wisdom.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Isaiah 2:6\",\n        \"Ezekiel 8:16\",\n        \"1 Kings 4:30\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"The symbolism of the East is dualistic and context-dependent. In early biblical narratives, traveling east frequently represents moving away from God's ideal into rebellion or exile, such as departing Eden or traveling toward Babylon and Sodom. Conversely, in prophetic and New Testament literature, the East often symbolizes hope, the dawning of divine light, the return of God's glory to the temple, and the eschatological return of Jesus.\"\n}",
